WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.690
Today on "Hello World,"

00:00:01.690 --> 00:00:04.770
we are sticking close to home for once.

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:07.260
We're gonna travel virtually down the road

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.470
to Stanford University,

00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:10.930
where there's a research lab

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:13.590
that is doing some
really cutting-edge work

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.410
around AI technology, video analysis,

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:19.370
and video manipulation.

00:00:19.370 --> 00:00:22.980
A group of researchers there
have done some projects

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:27.180
in things ranging from
creating fake tennis players,

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:30.180
versions of Roger Federer
and Serena Williams

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.800
that can play matches against each other.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:36.170
And they're also doing this
crazy analysis of cable news,

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:39.430
where they've had an AI
watch 10,000-something hours

00:00:39.430 --> 00:00:44.280
of cable TV news and find
out which topics dominate,

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:46.440
who's getting the most
airtime between people

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:47.660
like Trump and Biden.

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:50.730
And so, it's just, it's these
fascinating applications

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:54.500
of the cutting edge of
where AI and video meet.

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:57.600
Some of it, I think,
will blow your mind a bit

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.740
and raise some difficult questions.

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:04.003
And so, with that, let's head
off to Stanford University.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.930
Most generally I'm a professor
of computer graphics.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:14.260
And so, my students and
myself and colleagues

00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:17.190
like creating new interactive experiences

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:18.510
that were never possible before.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:21.710
That's sort of our mission,
what makes us tick.

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:25.220
I was on Twitter one day and
I'm just scrolling through.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:26.610
And I'm a tennis nerd,

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.930
and so, I caught a video of this thing

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:33.794
of Roger Federer playing
against himself at Wimbledon.

00:01:33.794 --> 00:01:36.210
And that obviously caught my attention

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:38.570
since that's kind of impossible.

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:41.390
And, you know, I started digging into it,

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:45.270
and I saw this was based
on some video analysis

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:49.660
and AI technology that you
guys have been working on.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:50.980
A lot of us in the world right now

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:53.120
are really interested in different forms

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:56.800
of generating, analyzing,
or manipulating video.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:01:59.100
And so, I actually think it
was an idea from my colleague.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:00.487
He said, "Hey, you should take

00:02:00.487 --> 00:02:02.747
"all this broadcast
sports video that's around

00:02:02.747 --> 00:02:05.827
"and make a really cool
video game out of it."

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:09.180
Yeah, and just to break it down,

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.590
and you tell me if I'm
understanding this right,

00:02:11.590 --> 00:02:15.510
but you feed your systems all this video,

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:18.420
just the raw video of
these matches being played.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:19.910
Each player that you're focused on,

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:22.200
you're learning their, the style of play,

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.060
what shots they're likely to hit.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:27.210
We took two ideas that are
out there in the real world.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:28.780
We took sports analytics,

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:32.580
which all the leagues
are doing all the time,

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:34.190
and that they're analyzing the video.

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.620
And then we took a very basic idea

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:40.160
from computer graphics circa 2000,

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.630
which is if you want a
character to do something,

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:44.430
record them doing many things,

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:45.860
and then mix and match

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:48.130
a bunch of little video clips together.

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:52.110
And we did use a number of
modern deep-learning techniques

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.890
to really fill in some of the gaps.

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:58.990
I mean, obviously, deep
fakes are a big concern

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:00.810
with modern AI.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.190
Are there, you know, legit, I don't know,

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:05.290
concerns that could arise
from something like this?

00:03:05.290 --> 00:03:08.180
I think there are absolutely
always legitimate concerns,

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:11.790
but a lot of what we did
was based on technology

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:14.828
that existed 15, 20 years ago.

00:03:14.828 --> 00:03:17.490
And I agree that there's
an inflection point.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:20.020
When it starts looking
real, very realistic,

00:03:20.020 --> 00:03:22.240
there's great power that comes with that.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:24.013
But it is not a new thing.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:26.373
For a while, you're like,

00:03:26.373 --> 00:03:28.184
oh wow, this is Roger
Federer hitting the ball.

00:03:28.184 --> 00:03:29.610
And then every now and then, you're like,

00:03:29.610 --> 00:03:30.630
Oh, it moves-

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:31.463
Absolutely, yeah.

00:03:31.463 --> 00:03:32.296
A little weird.

00:03:32.296 --> 00:03:34.400
I don't know if glitchy is the right word.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:36.720
But then at the same time,
I was like, you know,

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:38.750
I play tennis video games,

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:43.090
and there was a realism
to it that you don't see

00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:44.570
in a lot of those games.

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:46.620
You could see that this is, maybe,

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:48.590
the future of where this stuff is going,

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:52.070
is like I can actually play
a simulated Roger Federer.

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:53.780
I mean, absolutely.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:55.470
The next step going forward is,

00:03:55.470 --> 00:03:58.480
is working with folks
that can give us access

00:03:58.480 --> 00:03:59.600
to a lot more video,

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:02.340
'cause we did what we showed with only

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:05.230
two or three matches from
both of these players.

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:07.910
If you gave us a much bigger database

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:09.830
and the next set of techniques,

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:11.540
I believe we can generate

00:04:11.540 --> 00:04:13.689
a very compelling visual experience.

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:18.090
You guys do a variety of
different projects over there,

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:21.010
and you also have this
project where you've done

00:04:21.010 --> 00:04:25.410
this really broad analysis of cable news,

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:29.590
looking at both what is said
by analyzing transcripts

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:31.810
and also who is saying it.

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:34.830
Who's on TV the most
between men and women,

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:36.160
different pundits, you know,

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:37.280
different people in the news

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:39.480
like Trump and Biden, obviously.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.710
We're taking an extremely
large corpus of video.

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:44.960
We have almost 24/7 broadcasts

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:48.223
of CNN, MSNBC, and Fox News since 2010.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:52.560
So, we have transcripts and we
have video for all of those.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:54.900
it's provided by the Internet Archive.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:56.925
And what we've done is
we've basically turned it

00:04:56.925 --> 00:04:58.740
into an enormous library.

00:04:58.740 --> 00:05:01.040
We've indexed it and we allow the public

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:03.300
to search those transcripts,

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:06.320
as well as who is on screen.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:09.850
And so, we're really excited to see how,

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:13.100
if we give the public, whether
that be media watchdogs,

00:05:13.100 --> 00:05:14.420
whether that be journalists,

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:16.510
whether it be scholars or hobbyists,

00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:19.560
the ability to essentially
search this library

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:21.320
of who and what was on the news,

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:24.280
we think that some very
positive things might happen

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:26.810
in terms of understanding
what gets presented,

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:29.330
who gets the opportunity to present it,

00:05:29.330 --> 00:05:33.660
as well as what biases or

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:36.163
dispositions the various channels have.

00:05:36.163 --> 00:05:37.460
And so, I noticed,

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:38.293
I mean, obviously,

00:05:38.293 --> 00:05:40.520
historically there are watchdog groups

00:05:40.520 --> 00:05:41.967
that look at this sort of thing.

00:05:41.967 --> 00:05:44.440
But it's all, it's a
manual process, right?

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:47.659
Some poor human has to watch endless hours

00:05:47.659 --> 00:05:49.800
of horrendous cable news.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:50.633
That's correct.

00:05:50.633 --> 00:05:53.200
And the big difference
here is that we have taken

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.050
some very manual, painstaking labor

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:59.300
by folks that wanna understand
what's being communicated

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:02.083
and how to computer-automate
a lot of that labor.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:04.260
We are just a few blocks-

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:06.530
One of the realizations that came out

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:10.531
was an imbalance between
male and female hosts.

00:06:10.531 --> 00:06:12.860
There's a lot more male hosts.

00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:14.470
One thing I was surprised about was,

00:06:14.470 --> 00:06:19.090
I mean, Fox News has
almost a one-to-one ratio

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:21.700
between male hosts and female hosts.

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:24.570
They had by far the best
balance on the host side,

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:26.860
but on the pundit side,

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:28.620
they were, I think, the worst.

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:29.453
That's correct.

00:06:29.453 --> 00:06:32.210
And one of the real,

00:06:32.210 --> 00:06:33.800
the things that we're interested in doing

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:36.380
is that we all have a narrative

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:39.550
about what's going on in the world.

00:06:39.550 --> 00:06:43.160
And this is about putting
data behind that narrative.

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:46.550
And the conclusions, sometimes,
are quite interesting.

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:47.930
I wanted to talk just a little bit

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:50.780
about the nuts and bolts of
how you guys put this together,

00:06:50.780 --> 00:06:52.630
'cause one thing I did not realize,

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:56.120
Amazon has this facial recognition tool

00:06:56.120 --> 00:06:58.920
that you can buy as a service.

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:01.350
What we are surfacing to
the public are the results

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:04.670
of Amazon's celebrity
recognition service API.

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:06.910
I'm excited to introduce
for you a new service

00:07:06.910 --> 00:07:08.530
called Amazon Rekognition Video,

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:11.161
which does real time and
batch video analytics.

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:16.120
We'll detect objects and faces and scenes.

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:20.140
It is a form of doing
facial recognition at scale,

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:23.020
which has been talked about and
continues to be talked about

00:07:23.020 --> 00:07:24.490
at length in the media right now,

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:27.340
because it is an extremely
controversial technology.

00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:31.360
Amazon can scan your
face without your consent

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:33.580
and sell it to the government

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:35.610
all without our knowledge, correct?

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:36.500
Yes.

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:39.050
Now, on one hand, that's
what we are doing.

00:07:39.050 --> 00:07:42.470
We are running Amazon's
face recognition software

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:45.670
on every frame of the
news for the last decade.

00:07:45.670 --> 00:07:50.320
But we think that this is an
application of face recognition

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:53.500
where the potential for harm is low.

00:07:53.500 --> 00:07:55.200
It is run only on individuals

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:58.250
that have appeared on cable TV news.

00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:00.680
But, well, we thought that the ability

00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:03.110
to audit what is going on on the news

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:05.250
was a positive use case,

00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:06.470
but we had to make a judgment.

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:08.320
And the judgment we made
was that the benefits

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:10.950
outweighed the potential harms.

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:13.410
One thing I think is worth bringing up,

00:08:13.410 --> 00:08:14.890
which you guys address,

00:08:14.890 --> 00:08:16.370
the facial recognition.

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:19.620
It does way better on
kinda like white guys

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:21.280
than it does on females,

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:23.870
and it also has some struggles on gender,

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:28.870
especially for people who might
identify in different ways.

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:29.973
Is that right?

00:08:29.973 --> 00:08:31.030
I mean, it's basically strongest

00:08:31.030 --> 00:08:33.920
on white male pundit than anything else.

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:36.460
Most of these services
have shown scientifically

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:39.310
that there is bias in these systems,

00:08:39.310 --> 00:08:42.340
and so any conclusions that you take

00:08:42.340 --> 00:08:46.580
from automated machine learning analysis

00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:49.120
need to be inspected very carefully

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:51.940
to determine if that
bias is playing a factor

00:08:53.295 --> 00:08:54.433
in the results.

00:08:55.900 --> 00:09:00.070
Our goal is to create
interesting new capabilities,

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:02.800
tools for analysts or creators,

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:05.400
tools for artists, if
it's in entertainment.

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:08.390
And so, like any tool builder,

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:10.620
you wanna use the best tool for the job.

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:13.730
And when AI provides new opportunities

00:09:13.730 --> 00:09:15.790
and is the right tool for the job,

00:09:15.790 --> 00:09:17.750
by all means, we should use it.

00:09:17.750 --> 00:09:20.120
But we also look at it
with some skepticism

00:09:20.120 --> 00:09:22.650
in that if we go back
to the tennis project

00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:24.290
that we talked about earlier,

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:27.350
shoot, most of the reason
why that looks so good

00:09:27.350 --> 00:09:30.710
is because we took human
knowledge of how tennis works

00:09:30.710 --> 00:09:33.010
and encoded it in the computer,

00:09:33.010 --> 00:09:35.810
and we used the AI to fill in the gaps.

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:37.960
But the main knowledge is still human,

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:40.480
and I actually think when
people stop doing that

00:09:40.480 --> 00:09:42.580
and rely completely on data

00:09:42.580 --> 00:09:44.820
or completely on AI to build systems,

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:47.160
that's where stuff starts going wrong.

00:09:47.160 --> 00:09:49.690
You know, for better or for worse,

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:54.017
computing has gotten very, very
powerful in the last decade.

00:09:54.017 --> 00:09:58.640
And there are amazing things
that we can do with that power,

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:02.500
and there are significant
concerns and significant issues

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:06.190
that that power is bringing
to bear on all society.

00:10:06.190 --> 00:10:09.070
There's a lot of responsibility
to think very thoughtfully

00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:10.670
about what we are doing and why.

